WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.518 --> 00:00:01.560
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Dina.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.831
What are you doing?

00:00:02.831 --> 00:00:04.675
[ALARM RINGS]

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.590
DINA PORTMAN:
Well, the last time

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.260
we talked, we helped Katie
at CatTube use webhooks

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.650
to set up a system for
processing GitHub issues.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.180
I'm worried the
functions may not

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.110
complete before they time out,
or there might be an error

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.003
and the whole thing will fail.

00:00:21.003 --> 00:00:22.920
MARTIN OMANDER: I think
we have just the thing

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.383
to help Katie
improve her webhook.

00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:28.281
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.350
DINA PORTMAN: Katie's
webhook is currently

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.980
set up to create notifications,
trigger an action,

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.410
and stream data into BigQuery.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.060
MARTIN OMANDER: That's
a lot for one webhook.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.190
DINA PORTMAN: You're right.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.770
Her current system
works, but it's fragile.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:52.580
Everything is tangled together
and running synchronously.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.610
This means we're not returning
a response to GitHub until all

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.327
the functions have completed.

00:00:57.327 --> 00:00:58.910
MARTIN OMANDER:
That's risky business,

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:00.470
given that each
of these services

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.270
are constrained by timeouts.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.730
Cloud Run has a
15-minute timeout,

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.800
but GitHub wants
a valid response

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.650
within 10 seconds
of its request.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.320
Katie's request must be
completed by the shorter

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.970
of these two timeouts.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.710
DINA PORTMAN:
Katie will probably

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.760
be able to complete all three
functions in under 10 seconds.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.340
But I wouldn't want
to rely on "probably."

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.060
Luckily, we can use pub/sub
to asynchronously handle

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.300
the events from the GitHub API.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.670
Katie creates a new
Cloud Run endpoint

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.130
to be the initial
webhook target.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.840
This new endpoint will
verify the signature,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.620
publish the message to
pub/sub, and quickly return

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.970
a 200 OK status message
to GitHub, thus allowing

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:47.545
the rest of the work to be
processed asynchronously.

00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:48.920
MARTIN OMANDER:
But once you send

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.490
the message to pub/sub, how do
you trigger those functions?

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.430
DINA PORTMAN: Well, we need
to create another service

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.670
and have it subscribed
to the pub/sub topic.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.130
This way, it'll be triggered
whenever a message is

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.030
posted to the topic.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.140
For additional security,
when we create the service,

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.630
we should allow only
authorized requests

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:12.560
and set up pub/sub to send
us authorization tokens.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.470
Here, we can look at our
reconfigured Slack function.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:17.630
It's confirming that
the data it receives

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.510
looks like a pub/sub
message, and then triggers

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.510
the notification to Slack.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.200
Thanks to our
setup, we don't have

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.450
to worry about authorization
or signature verification.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.880
Our architecture looks a
little more complicated now,

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.100
but we've solved our
issue with the timeouts.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.730
And as an added bonus,
this kind of decoupling

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.745
creates a system that is
better able to handle errors.

00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:43.370
MARTIN OMANDER: What
do you mean, Dina?

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.400
DINA PORTMAN: Well, by creating
separate services for our three

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.340
different functions, we
ensure that if one service has

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.770
an error the other
two are not affected.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:56.982
This is one of the benefits of
a microservice architecture.

00:02:56.982 --> 00:02:58.190
MARTIN OMANDER: That's great.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.180
But what do we do about the
errors in the broken service?

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.150
DINA PORTMAN: If the message
is not successfully delivered,

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.360
pub/sub will keep retrying
until it succeeds.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.140
Once we have the
service back online,

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.520
the messages will be processed
through our healthy service.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.760
MARTIN OMANDER: So before,
Katie's webhook target worked,

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.590
but it was
synchronously triggering

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.330
three distinct functions.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.580
Meaning it was at
risk for timeouts,

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.220
and for one error
in one function

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.665
taking down the whole service.

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:28.790
DINA PORTMAN: That's right.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.130
But now that Katie has
rewritten her webhook

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:33.590
to send all events
through pub/sub,

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:37.550
she has a more robust and
fault-tolerant webhook target

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.355
ready to take on the world.

00:03:39.355 --> 00:03:40.730
MARTIN OMANDER:
Thanks to pub/sub

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.820
and a microservice
architecture, Katie

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:46.610
can rest assured that her team
isn't missing any notifications

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:47.900
or losing any data.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:51.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

